 Grade Safari chapter TruckBody against wasm/truck.zig's drawBody.

 A REAL ORACLE, and a stronger one than the drawing seams that came before it.
 drawBody is `pub` -- render.zig's own comment says so and names this as the
 reason -- so the probe calls the game's function and reads the words it wrote on
 paint's wire. `render.at` is pub too, and it is the only render entry drawBody
 touches, so unlike RenderCheck nothing on this seam is graded against a mirror of
 something private. PORTING_NOTES E2 is the rule and this is the happy case of it.

 WHAT A COMMAND SEQUENCE ASSERTS THAT A PICTURE DOES NOT. The order of these 113
 commands is the depth sort, the sixteen faces, and the three-phase sequence --
 beams, body, lights -- all at once. A face sorted one place wrong shows up as two
 swapped colours in `tb-col` long before any coordinate moves, and a beam pushed
 after the body instead of before it moves a tag 4 past sixteen tag 0s.

 THE SIX CASES ARE BRANCHES:

   d = 0 mid-distance      sixteen opaque faces and nothing else -- the shape of
                           a plain frame, and the only case with no gradient.
   the same, lit + braking  the full sequence: two wedges, the body, then two
                           halo-and-core pairs. 24 commands where the first was 16.
   d = 1 across a 50 turn   the join composition. A wrong `at` bends the box
                           around its neighbour's turn instead of its own, which
                           is exactly the failure Render's prose warns is
                           invisible as corruption and plausible as a road.
   d = 2 from segment 3     two joins deep, rider off centre and yawed, so the
                           pose transform and the composition are both live.
   fifteen metres ahead     near geometry: large screen coordinates and beams
                           that fan across most of the canvas.
   two metres ahead         THE CLIPPING CASE, and it is the one that earns its
                           place. The trailer rear is BEHIND the eye, so the near
                           clip fires on the faces, and the brake panel -- which
                           lives on that rear face -- fails the wedge/halo
                           behind-test outright. Both brake commands and both
                           halos are simply absent, and the port has to be absent
                           in the same places. Two structural streams shorter is
                           the assertion; nothing about it is a tolerance.

 STRUCTURE EXACTLY. Tags, both colour words and every point count compare with no
 tolerance at all, which is D4. The gradient's own three numbers -- the lamp's x
 and y and the radius -- get their own stream rather than riding with the polygon,
 so a beam that kept its outline and lost its centre fails on `tb-geom` and names
 itself.

 THE COORDINATE GATE IS FIFTEEN TIMES LOOSER THAN EVERY OTHER SCREEN SEAM IN THIS
 PORT, AND THE CLIPPING CASE IS WHY. 1.5e-5 relative against the 1e-6 the cat, the
 animals and the rails are graded at, and it is measured rather than chosen: the
 stream reddens at 7.5e-6 and passes above it, on one vertex -- a tire in the
 two-metres-ahead case, clipped against the near plane.

 It is D3's cancellation argument in a third place, and the ratio is arithmetic
 rather than a mystery. A clipped vertex sits at forward = near = 0.4 m exactly,
 so the projection divides by 0.4 and multiplies by the 685 px focal: a 1,713x
 amplification of an error in metres. The error in metres is one f32 ulp at the
 coordinate the rider transform passes through -- his own `along` of 100 m, where
 an ulp is 7.6e-6 m -- because the port carries that subtraction in f64 and the
 game in f32. 7.6e-6 m x 1713 is 0.013 px, against an x of about -1300, which is
 1e-5 relative. The measurement and the prediction agree to within the width of
 the bisection.

 So the loosening is the near plane's, not the truck's: the same seam grades at
 1e-6 in every case where nothing is clipped. Dropping the clipping case would buy
 back the tighter gate and lose the only structural assertion about what happens
 when the chase closes, which is a bad trade.

 THE FLOOR IS THE STANDARD THOUSANDTH OF A PIXEL AND IT IS NOT LOAD-BEARING HERE:
 every coordinate in these six cases is hundreds of pixels or more, and the stream
 passes with the floor set to zero. It is kept because the seam can produce a
 coordinate near zero and a bare relative gate has no answer for one. On `tb-geom`
 the floor IS the governing half -- a beam's radius is 4.6 px in the far cases,
 where a relative gate is worth 5e-6 -- and that stream's worst absolute
 disagreement is under 5e-4 px, so it reddens within 2x of its setting.

 We say:

Section: The Cases

  TbCase = record {
    start : Integer,
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    d : Integer,
    center : Real,
    braking : Boolean,
    headlights : Boolean
  }

  tb-cases : List TbCase
  tb-cases = [TbCase { start = 0, along = 100.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, d = 0, center = 250.0, braking = False, headlights = False }, TbCase { start = 0, along = 100.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, d = 0, center = 250.0, braking = True, headlights = True }, TbCase { start = 0, along = 460.0, across = 0.8, yaw = 0.0 - 0.06, d = 1, center = 160.0, braking = True, headlights = True }, TbCase { start = 2, along = 50.0, across = 0.0 - 1.1, yaw = 0.12, d = 2, center = 200.0, braking = False, headlights = False }, TbCase { start = 0, along = 100.0, across = 0.3, yaw = 0.0, d = 0, center = 115.0, braking = True, headlights = True }, TbCase { start = 0, along = 100.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, d = 0, center = 102.0, braking = True, headlights = True }]

 THE WORLD IS THREADED, not mentioned. A nullary binding emits as a function and
 rebuilds all nineteen segments at every mention (PORTING_NOTES B13); each case
 below mentions it four times.

  tb-pose : List Segment, List Integer, TbCase -> Pose
  tb-pose (w) (ch) (c) =
    Pose { along = c.along, across = c.across, yaw = c.yaw, hw = (list-at w (list-at ch 0)).width / 2.0 }

 The half width handed to the body is the width of the segment the truck's CENTRE
 is on, which is chain index d and not the rider's own. On this route they are the
 same number, and passing the rider's would still be wrong.

  tb-wire-of : List Segment, TbCase -> List DrawCmd
  tb-wire-of (w) (c) =
    let ch = build-chain w c.start
    in let hw = (list-at w (list-at ch c.d)).width / 2.0
    in truck-draw-body w ch (tb-pose w ch c) c.d c.center hw c.braking c.headlights focal camera-w

  tb-wire : List Segment, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tb-wire (w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length tb-cases then []
    else (tb-wire-of w (list-at tb-cases i)) & tb-wire w (i + 1)

  all-cmds : List DrawCmd
  all-cmds = tb-wire build-world 0

Section: Flattening

  tb-tags : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  tb-tags (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).tag] & tb-tags cs (i + 1)

  tb-cols : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  tb-cols (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).color] & tb-cols cs (i + 1)

  tb-cols2 : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  tb-cols2 (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).color2] & tb-cols2 cs (i + 1)

  tb-counts : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  tb-counts (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [list-length (list-at cs i).pts / 2] & tb-counts cs (i + 1)

 Only tag 4 defines `geom`, so concatenating every command's is the gradient
 stream and nothing else -- the solid fills contribute an empty list each.

  tb-geoms : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Real
  tb-geoms (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (list-at cs i).geom & tb-geoms cs (i + 1)

  tb-coords : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Real
  tb-coords (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (list-at cs i).pts & tb-coords cs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tb-tag  " (tb-tags all-cmds 0) g-tb-tag)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tb-col  " (tb-cols all-cmds 0) g-tb-col)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tb-col2 " (tb-cols2 all-cmds 0) g-tb-col2)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tb-cnt  " (tb-counts all-cmds 0) g-tb-cnt)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "tb-geom " (tb-geoms all-cmds 0) g-tb-geom 0.001 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "tb-xy   " (tb-coords all-cmds 0) g-tb-xy 0.001 0.000015)
  end
